  "Number": 7,
  "Question": "If \\(\\dfrac{3}{5}=\\dfrac{M}{45}=\\dfrac{60}{N},\\) what is \\(M+N?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\( \\ 27 \\)",
    "B": "\\( \\ 29 \\)",
    "C": "\\( \\ 45 \\)",
    "D": "\\( \\ 105 \\)",
    "E": "\\( \\ 127 \\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "We can match each known entry by multiplying the numerator and denominator of \\(3/5\\) by the same nonzero factor, which preserves the fraction's value.\n\nSince \\(45=5\\cdot9\\), multiply both parts by \\(9\\):\n\\[\n\\frac35=\\frac{3\\cdot9}{5\\cdot9}=\\frac{27}{45}.\n\\]\nThus \\(M=27\\).\n\nTo make the numerator \\(60\\), use \\(60=3\\cdot20\\) and multiply both parts by \\(20\\):\n\\[\n\\frac35=\\frac{3\\cdot20}{5\\cdot20}=\\frac{60}{100}.\n\\]\nThus \\(N=100\\), a nonzero denominator as required. Therefore\n\\[\nM+N=27+100=\\boxed{127},\n\\]\nwhich is choice \\(\\text{E}\\).",
